-- title:
--   §4.1, p. 17 — the SDE (2) has a unique global strong solution
-- statement:
--   Let $f(x)_i=\sum_{j,k}c_{ijk}x_jx_k$ be a quadratic drift with $x\cdot f(x)=0$ for all $x\in\mathbb R^N$, let $q>0$, let $W$ be an $N$-dimensional standard Wiener process on a probability space $(\Omega,P)$, and let $\xi$ be a random vector in $\mathbb R^N$ independent of $W$. Then there is a process $X$ such that
--   1. for every horizon $T\ge0$, $X$ solves
--   $$dX(t)=f(X(t))\,dt+\sqrt q\,dW(t),\qquad X(0)=\xi,$$
--   pathwise on $[0,T]$ (continuous paths, integral equation almost surely);
--   2. each $X(t)$ coincides almost surely with a function of $\xi$ and $(W(s))_{s\le t}$ (a strong solution);
--   3. every process $X'$ that solves the equation on every horizon agrees with $X$: almost surely, $X'(t)=X(t)$ for all $t\ge0$.
--
--   This is the well-posedness of APX-SDE: the expected value $\mathbb E\,u_0(X(T))$ refers to this solution.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: f ∈ 𝒟 (locally Lipschitz with x · f(x) = 0), q ∈ (0, 1], initial condition Y + √p Z → any quadratic drift with x · f(x) = 0 (symmetry and volume preservation not assumed), any q > 0, any measurable initial vector ξ independent of W; reason: the sentence uses only local Lipschitzness and x · f(x) = 0; the source's case is a special case
--   - reading: unique global strong solution → a process solving the integral equation on every horizon, with X(t) a.e. measurable w.r.t. σ(ξ, W(s) : s ≤ t), unique a.s. at all times among all pathwise solutions on every horizon; reason: additive noise needs no stochastic integral; uniqueness among not-necessarily-adapted pathwise solutions contains uniqueness among strong ones
--   - encoding: indices in [N], time t ≥ 0 → Fin N (0-based), process time ℝ≥0, ODE time ℝ on [0, T]; reason: Lean convention; same instances
-- source:
--   Bravyi, Byrne, Zayats, Zhuk, Zlokapa, Quantum algorithms for stochastic nonlinear differential equations, arXiv:2606.08349 (sha256 29bd9b01…94cdf), §4.1, p. 17 (unnumbered; citing [GK21, Remark 2.5 and Corollary 2.6])

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Peng1990_SMP_Stochastic
import Definitions.Def_QuadSDE_DriftClass
import Definitions.Def_QuadSDE_NoisySDE

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal

namespace QuadSDE

/-- **§4.1, p. 17 (unnumbered): the SDE has a unique global strong solution.**
Bravyi–Byrne–Zayats–Zhuk–Zlokapa, arXiv:2606.08349 (sha256 29bd9b01…), §4.1, p. 17: "Let `X(t)` be
the solution of the SDE Eq. (2). Since `f` is locally Lipschitz and `x · f(x) = 0`, the SDE has a
unique global strong solution [GK21, Remark 2.5 and Corollary 2.6]."

Let `f` be a quadratic drift with `x · f(x) = 0` for all `x`, let `q > 0`, let `W` be an
`N`-dimensional standard Wiener process on a probability space, and let `ξ` be a random initial
vector independent of `W`. Then there is a process `X` such that
1. for every horizon `T`, `X` solves `dX = f(X) dt + √q dW`, `X(0) = ξ`, on `[0, T]`;
2. every `X(t)` is (up to a null set) a function of `ξ` and `(W(s))_{s ≤ t}` (strong solution);
3. every other process solving the equation on every horizon agrees with `X` almost surely, at all
   times simultaneously.

**Formalization Note.** Solutions are pathwise (`SolvesPathwise`). Adaptedness is
`AEStronglyMeasurable` with respect to the σ-algebra generated by `ξ` and `W(s)`, `s ≤ t`. The
hypotheses are only the norm-preservation condition (6) on a quadratic (hence locally Lipschitz)
drift, which is what the sentence uses; the initial vector is any random vector independent of `W`
(the paper's is `Y + √p Z`). -/
theorem sec_4_1_well_posed {N : ℕ} (c : Coeff N)
    (hnorm : ∀ x : Fin N → ℝ, ∑ i, x i * drift c x i = 0)
    (q : ℝ) (hq : 0 < q) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Fin N → ℝ) (hB : Peng1990.SMP.IsStdBrownian P B)
    (ξ : Ω → Fin N → ℝ) (hξ : Measurable ξ) (hind : IndepFun ξ (fun ω t => B t ω) P) :
    ∃ X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Fin N → ℝ,
      (∀ T : ℝ≥0, SolvesPathwise P (drift c) q B ξ X T) ∧
      (∀ t : ℝ≥0, AEStronglyMeasurable[MeasurableSpace.comap ξ inferInstance ⊔
          ⨆ s : ℝ≥0, ⨆ (_ : s ≤ t), MeasurableSpace.comap (B s) inferInstance] (X t) P) ∧
      ∀ X' : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Fin N → ℝ, (∀ T : ℝ≥0, SolvesPathwise P (drift c) q B ξ X' T) →
        ∀ᵐ ω ∂P, ∀ t : ℝ≥0, X' t ω = X t ω := by sorry

end QuadSDE
